Part 1: Git Bash commands
Open Git Bash and run the commands one by one.

1. Clone your repository
git clone YOUR-REPOSITORY-URL
cd Gitbash-exp1-


If the repository is already cloned, skip this step.

2. Create a file and push it
echo "Hello GitHub" > file1.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git push origin main


3. Create a branch and push changes
git checkout -b feature1
echo "Feature update" >> file1.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Feature update"
git push -u origin feature1


4. Compare and merge branches
git diff main feature1
git checkout main
git merge feature1
git push origin main


5. Demonstrate merge conflict
git checkout -b test
echo "Test branch" > file1.txt
git add . && git commit -m "Test change"
git checkout main
echo "Main branch" > file1.txt
git add . && git commit -m "Main change"
git merge test


When Git reports a conflict, open file1.txt, remove the conflict markers, save the file, and run:
git add file1.txt
git commit -m "Resolve conflict"
git push origin main


Part 2: GitHub website
6. Fork and create a Pull Request
1. Open Hello-World.
2. Click Fork. If you already forked it, use your existing fork.
3. Open README.md in your fork and click the pencil icon.
4. Add a line and click Commit changes.
5. Click Contribute → Open pull request → Create pull request.

What to do next
1. Keep the title as Update README.
2. In the description box, type This is my DevOps lab experiment. (optional).
3. Scroll down.
4. Click the green Create pull request button.
Expected final output
GitHub will open a page showing your Pull Request with an Open status.


